      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я переживаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I worry.' Vika starts with 'I' and pairs it with 'worry' to make the point clear. And Max jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Не переживай так.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not worry so much.' Notice the word order: 'worry' comes first, and 'worry' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Почему?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why?' Here 'why' sets up the idea, while 'why' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Главное, что мы вместе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The main thing is that we are together.' The question word for 'what' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'together'. Vika answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это главное.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is the main thing.' The key word here means 'this', which Vika combines with 'important'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Кому-то проще потерять все.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'For some it is easier to lose everything.' Notice the word order: 'someone' comes first, and 'to lose' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Зачем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What for?' Vika puts 'what for' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'what for'. Then Max replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Чтобы понять.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In order to understand.' The first content word means 'to understand', and 'to understand' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Понять что?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'To understand what?' The first content word means 'to understand', and 'what' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Насколько это было дорого.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How dear it was.' In Russian, 'as far as' leads into 'expensive' in a very natural everyday phrasing. And Vika jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нам не надо терять.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We do not need to lose.' Russian expresses this impersonally, literally saying 'to me' or 'to you' with 'to lose'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Не надо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No need.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'it is necessary' straight to 'it is necessary'.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
